  "activity": {
    "id": "retrograde_motion_activity",
    "name": "Exploring Retrograde Motion: From Observation to Heliocentric Explanation",
    "description": "A comprehensive AR lab where students investigate apparent retrograde motion of Mars through observation, historical models, and physical demonstration. Students begin by observing Mars's retrograde loop in the night sky, explore the geocentric epicycle explanation, visualize the heliocentric model where Earth overtakes Mars, compare outer planets, and make predictions about retrograde configurations.",
    "workplace": "retrograde_motion_workspace",
    "start": "step_1_night_sky_observation",
    "actions": [
      {
        "id": "step_1_night_sky_observation",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "The Puzzle: Mars in Retrograde",
          "description": "Stand on Earth and observe the night sky over six months. Watch Mars drift slowly eastward through the constellations Leo, Virgo, and Libra. At a certain point, Mars will slow down, stop, and reverse its motion\u2014moving westward in a loop\u2014before resuming eastward motion. This retrograde behavior is the central puzzle of planetary astronomy. Why would Mars move backward? Your challenge: observe the complete path and describe the motion pattern."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "night_sky_location",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "mars_retrograde_trail"
            },
            {
              "target": "night_sky_location",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "constellation_labels"
            },
            {
              "target": "mars_planet",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "retrograde_narration_intro"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "mars_planet",
              "augmentation": "retrograde_narration_intro"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_1_night_sky_observation"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_2_geocentric_model",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Historical Solution: The Epicycle Model",
          "description": "The ancient and medieval astronomers proposed the geocentric model: Earth sits at the center, and Mars rides on a small circle called an epicycle. This epicycle itself rides on a larger circle (the deferent) centered on Earth. Watch the machinery in motion. When Mars reaches the inner part of its epicycle\u2014the side closest to Earth\u2014its combined motion makes it appear to move backward. This model successfully predicted the retrograde loop, but it required carefully tuned parameters. As observations improved, more epicycles had to be added to match the data. Is there a simpler explanation?"
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "model_stage",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "geocentric_epicycle_animation",
              "poi": "geocentric_view"
            },
            {
              "target": "epicycle_gear",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "epicycle_label"
            },
            {
              "target": "epicycle_gear",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "deferent_label"
            },
            {
              "target": "model_stage",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "geocentric_explanation_narration"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "model_stage",
              "augmentation": "geocentric_explanation_narration"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_2_geocentric_model"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_3_heliocentric_model",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "The Heliocentric Revolution: Simpler Geometry",
          "description": "Now view the solar system from above. The Sun sits at the center. Earth orbits at 1 AU with a period of one year; Mars orbits at 1.52 AU with a period of 1.88 years. Watch both planets run their orbits at correct relative speeds. Notice that Earth, moving faster on its inner orbit, periodically catches up to and overtakes Mars\u2014roughly every 26 months. When Earth passes Mars, what appears to be a retrograde loop from Earth's perspective is simply the natural result of relative motion. No epicycles needed. The heliocentric model explains the phenomenon with just two smooth ellipses."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "model_stage",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "heliocentric_orbit_animation",
              "poi": "heliocentric_view"
            },
            {
              "target": "sun",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "mars_position_indicator"
            },
            {
              "target": "model_stage",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "heliocentric_explanation_narration"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "model_stage",
              "augmentation": "heliocentric_explanation_narration"
            },
            {
              "target": "sun",
              "augmentation": "mars_position_indicator"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_3_heliocentric_model"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_4_line_of_sight_linkage",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Connecting the Views: Line of Sight",
          "description": "A split-screen view now links the two perspectives. On the left, the heliocentric orbits of Earth and Mars; on the right, Earth's night sky showing the constellations. A line is drawn from Earth through Mars and extended to the distant stars. Watch as the planets move: during normal motion, this line sweeps eastward through the constellations. But when Earth begins to overtake Mars on the inside track, the line reverses and sweeps westward\u2014and at that exact moment, Mars's position in the night sky retraces its path. The retrograde loop is a real apparent motion, produced by the geometry of orbital mechanics, not a trick of the eye or a flaw in observation."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "night_sky_location",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "line_of_sight_sweep"
            },
            {
              "target": "model_stage",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "heliocentric_orbit_animation"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "night_sky_location",
              "augmentation": "line_of_sight_sweep"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_4_line_of_sight_linkage"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_5_outer_planets_comparison",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "The Pattern Across Planets: Size Depends on Distance",
          "description": "Return to the heliocentric view with all outer planets visible: Earth, Mars (at 1.52 AU), Jupiter (at 5.2 AU), and Saturn (at 9.5 AU). Step through a retrograde event for each planet and measure the angular size of each retrograde loop. Mars's loop spans about 15 degrees across the sky. Jupiter's loop is about 10 degrees. Saturn's is about 7 degrees. Why? More distant planets subtend a smaller angle in Earth's sky. When you move your head a few feet, a nearby object shifts more against distant background than a faraway object does\u2014same principle. As planets orbit farther from the Sun, Earth's orbital motion becomes a smaller fraction of the total distance, producing smaller apparent loops."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "model_stage",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "heliocentric_orbit_animation"
            },
            {
              "target": "mars_planet",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "mars_position_indicator"
            },
            {
              "target": "model_stage",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "outer_planets_comparison_narration"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "model_stage",
              "augmentation": "outer_planets_comparison_narration"
            },
            {
              "target": "mars_planet",
              "augmentation": "mars_position_indicator"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_5_outer_planets_comparison"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
